Generics in C# allow you to define classes, interfaces, methods, and delegates with placeholders for data types. This allows you to create reusable code that can work with different data types without having to explicitly specify them each time.

Generics are defined using angle brackets <> and a placeholder type parameter. For example, you can define a generic class like this:

```
public class GenericClass<T>
{
    public T data;

    public GenericClass(T value)
    {
        data = value;
    }
}
```

In this example, the `GenericClass` can work with any data type because the `T` placeholder can be replaced with any actual data type. You can then create instances of the `GenericClass` with specific data types like this:

```
GenericClass<int> intInstance = new GenericClass<int>(10);
GenericClass<string> stringInstance = new GenericClass<string>("Hello");
```

This allows you to write generic code that can be reused with different data types, providing greater flexibility and code reuse in your C# programs.

Here is a sample code in C# that demonstrates the described scenario:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.IO;
using System.Threading;

class Worker
{
    private object locker = new object();
    private Queue<string> linesQueue = new Queue<string>();
    private bool isShutdown = false;

    public void Start()
    {
        Thread workerThread = new Thread(() =>
        {
            while (true)
            {
                string line = null;
                lock(locker)
                {
                    if (linesQueue.Count > 0)
                    {
                        line = linesQueue.Dequeue();
                    }
                    else if (isShutdown)
                    {
                        break;
                    }
                }

                if (line != null)
                {
                    // Do some work with the line
                    Console.WriteLine(line);
                }
            }
        });

        workerThread.Start();
    }

    public void EnqueueLine(string line)
    {
        lock(locker)
        {
            linesQueue.Enqueue(line);
        }
    }

    public void Shutdown()
    {
        lock(locker)
        {
            isShutdown = true;
        }
    }
}

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        List<Worker> workers = new List<Worker>();
        for (int i = 0; i < 32; i++)
        {
            Worker worker = new Worker();
            workers.Add(worker);
            worker.Start();
        }

        string[] lines = File.ReadAllLines("textfile.txt");
        foreach (var line in lines)
        {
            Worker availableWorker = null;
            do
            {
                availableWorker = workers.Find(worker => worker != null && !worker.IsBusy());
                if(availableWorker == null)
                {
                    Thread.Sleep(100);
                }
            } while (availableWorker == null);

            availableWorker.EnqueueLine(line);
        }

        foreach (var worker in workers)
        {
            worker.Shutdown();
        }

        foreach (var worker in workers)
        {
            worker.WaitForShutdown();
        }
    }
}
```

In this code, we have a `Worker` class that represents a worker thread that processes lines. We create 32 worker threads in the main thread and then iterate through the lines in the text file, assigning each line to an available worker thread. After all lines have been processed, we shut down each worker thread and wait for them to finish processing any remaining lines before the main thread exits.

Here is a simple Java code snippet that reverses a string:

```java
public class StringReversal {

    public static String reverseString(String input) {
        char[] charArray = input.toCharArray();
        int i = 0;
        int j = input.length() - 1;

        while (i < j) {
            char temp = charArray[i];
            charArray[i] = charArray[j];
            charArray[j] = temp;
            i++;
            j--;
        }

        return new String(charArray);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String originalString = "Hello, world!";
        String reversedString = reverseString(originalString);

        System.out.println("Original string: " + originalString);
        System.out.println("Reversed string: " + reversedString);
    }
}
```

When you run the `main` method, it will print out the original string "Hello, world!" and its reversed form "!dlrow ,olleH".

