```javascript
const apiUrls = ["https://api.example.com/1", "https://api.example.com/2", "https://api.example.com/3"];

const apiPromises = apiUrls.map(url => fetch(url).then(response => response.json()));

Promise.all(apiPromises)
  .then(results => {
    results.forEach((result, index) => {
      console.log(`API call ${index + 1} result:`, result);
    });
  })
  .catch(error => {
    console.error('Error fetching API:', error);
  });
```

In the example above, we have an array `apiUrls` containing the URLs of the API calls. We use `map()` to create an array of Promises by fetching each URL and parsing the JSON response. Once all Promises have resolved, we can access their results in the `results` array and log them to the console.

Make sure to handle any potential errors using the `catch()` method to log them to the console if any of the API calls fail.

