To generate a scaffold for a react-native C++ binding, you can follow these steps:

1. Create a new react-native project: 

```
npx react-native init MyReactNativeApp
cd MyReactNativeApp
```

2. Create a new C++ module for your binding:

```
mkdir MyCPlusPlusModule
cd MyCPlusPlusModule
```

3. Inside the `MyCPlusPlusModule` directory, create a new C++ source file for your module, for example `MyModule.cpp`:

```cpp
#include <jsi/jsi.h>

using namespace facebook;

void MyModuleFunction(const jsi::Runtime &runtime, const jsi::Value &thisValue, const jsi::Value *arguments, size_t count) {
  // Implement your C++ function here
}

void installMyModule(jsi::Runtime &runtime) {
  auto object = jsi::Object::createFromHostObject(runtime, {
    "MyFunction", jsi::Function::createFromHostFunction(runtime, jsi::PropNameID::forAscii(runtime, "MyFunction"), 0, MyModuleFunction),
  });
  runtime.global().setProperty(runtime, "MyModule", std::move(object));
}

```

4. Update the `MyModule.cpp` file with your desired C++ logic.

5. Add the C++ module to the React Native project by creating a new C++ module in the `MyReactNativeApp` project.

6. Edit the `MyReactNativeApp/android/app/src/main/jni/first-react-app.cpp` file to include the C++ module:

```cpp
#include "MyModule.cpp"
// Other C++ module includes

extern "C" JNIEXPORT void JNICALL Java_com_firstreactapp_MainActivity_initHybrid(JNIEnv* env, jobject obj) {
  // Initialize your C++ modules here
  installMyModule(jsi::Runtime::buildBareRuntime());
  // Initialize other C++ modules
}
```

7. In your React Native project, import the C++ module in your JavaScript code:

```jsx
import {MyModule} from 'react-native';

MyModule.MyFunction();
```

8. Run your React Native project:

```
npx react-native run-android
```

This is a basic scaffold for creating a React Native C++ binding. You can expand on this scaffold by adding more C++ modules and functions as needed for your project.

