model_response:
To ensure type safety while allowing datasets of different types to be managed within the same SortingSystem, we can implement the Dataset interface using Kotlin's generics feature. By using generics, we can create a Dataset interface that is parameterized by the type of the elements it contains.

Here is one possible implementation of the Dataset interface using generics:

```kotlin
interface Dataset<T> {
    fun add(element: T)
    fun remove(element: T)
    fun get(index: Int): T
    fun size(): Int
}

class SortingSystem {
    private val datasets = mutableMapOf<String, Dataset<*>>()

    fun <T> addDataset(name: String, dataset: Dataset<T>) {
        datasets[name] = dataset
    }

    fun getDataset(name: String): Dataset<*>? {
        return datasets[name]
    }
}

class IntegerDataset : Dataset<Int> {
    private val elements = mutableListOf<Int>()

    override fun add(element: Int) {
        elements.add(element)
    }

    override fun remove(element: Int) {
        elements.remove(element)
    }

    override fun get(index: Int): Int {
        return elements[index]
    }

    override fun size(): Int {
        return elements.size
    }
}

class StringDataset : Dataset<String> {
    private val elements = mutableListOf<String>()

    override fun add(element: String) {
        elements.add(element)
    }

    override fun remove(element: String) {
        elements.remove(element)
    }

    override fun get(index: Int): String {
        return elements[index]
    }

    override fun size(): Int {
        return elements.size
    }
}

fun main() {
    val sortingSystem = SortingSystem()

    val integerDataset = IntegerDataset()
    integerDataset.add(1)
    integerDataset.add(2)
    integerDataset.add(3)

    val stringDataset = StringDataset()
    stringDataset.add("hello")
    stringDataset.add("world")

    sortingSystem.addDataset("integers", integerDataset)
    sortingSystem.addDataset("strings", stringDataset)

    val retrievedIntegerDataset = sortingSystem.getDataset("integers") as Dataset<Int>
    val retrievedStringDataset = sortingSystem.getDataset("strings") as Dataset<String>

    println(retrievedIntegerDataset.get(0))
    println(retrievedStringDataset.get(0))
}
```

In this implementation, we have defined a Dataset interface that is parameterized by a type T. We have also created two concrete implementations of the Dataset interface: IntegerDataset and StringDataset. The SortingSystem class uses a map to store datasets with their respective names. The addDataset method adds a dataset to the system, and the getDataset method retrieves a dataset by its name.

By using generics, we can ensure type safety while allowing datasets of different types to be managed within the same SortingSystem. Each dataset implementation can safely handle elements of its corresponding type, and the SortingSystem class can manage datasets of different types without compromising type safety.

#include <stdio.h>

double custom_log(double x) {
    double sum = 0.0;
    double term = (x - 1) / (x + 1);
    double power = term;
    int i = 1;

    while(power > 0.000001 || power < -0.000001) {
        sum += power / i;
        power = power * term * term;
        i += 2;
    }

    return 2 * sum;
}

int main() {
    double num = 10.0;
    double result = custom_log(num);

    printf("The log of %lf is %lf\n", num, result);

    return 0;
}

import numpy as np
from numba import jit
import threading

@jit
def is_prime(n):
    if n <= 1:
        return False
    if n <= 3:
        return True
    if n % 2 == 0 or n % 3 == 0:
        return False
    i = 5
    while i * i <= n:
        if n % i == 0 or n % (i + 2) == 0:
            return False
        i += 6
    return True

@jit
def mersenne_prime(n):
    return 2**n - 1

@jit(nopython=True)
def perfect_number(n):
    p = mersenne_prime(n)
    perfect = (2**(n-1)) * p
    return perfect

def find_perfect_numbers(N):
    perfect_numbers = []
    n = 2
    count = 0
    while count < N:
        p = mersenne_prime(n)
        if is_prime(p):
            perfect_numbers.append(perfect_number(n))
            count += 1
        n += 1
    return perfect_numbers

N = 5
perfect_numbers = find_perfect_numbers(N)
print(perfect_numbers)

