Yes, the Chain of Responsibility design pattern can be implemented in C# by creating a chain of objects that can handle a request. Each object in the chain has a reference to the next object in the chain, and if one object is unable to handle the request, it passes it on to the next object in the chain until the request is handled or reaches the end of the chain.

Here is an example of implementing the Chain of Responsibility design pattern in C#:

```csharp
using System;

// Handler interface
public interface IHandler
{
    IHandler SetNext(IHandler handler);
    string HandleRequest(int request);
}

// Concrete handlers
public class Handler1 : IHandler
{
    private IHandler _nextHandler;

    public IHandler SetNext(IHandler handler)
    {
        _nextHandler = handler;
        return handler;
    }

    public string HandleRequest(int request)
    {
        if (request < 10)
        {
            return $"Handler 1: Handling request {request}";
        }
        else if (_nextHandler != null)
        {
            return _nextHandler.HandleRequest(request);
        }
        else
        {
            return "Request cannot be handled";
        }
    }
}

public class Handler2 : IHandler
{
    private IHandler _nextHandler;

    public IHandler SetNext(IHandler handler)
    {
        _nextHandler = handler;
        return handler;
    }

    public string HandleRequest(int request)
    {
        if (request >= 10 && request < 20)
        {
            return $"Handler 2: Handling request {request}";
        }
        else if (_nextHandler != null)
        {
            return _nextHandler.HandleRequest(request);
        }
        else
        {
            return "Request cannot be handled";
        }
    }
}

// Client code
class Program
{
    static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        // Create handlers
        var handler1 = new Handler1();
        var handler2 = new Handler2();

        // Set the chain of responsibility
        handler1.SetNext(handler2);

        // Process requests
        Console.WriteLine(handler1.HandleRequest(5));
        Console.WriteLine(handler1.HandleRequest(15));
        Console.WriteLine(handler1.HandleRequest(25));
    }
}
```

In this example, we have two concrete handler classes `Handler1` and `Handler2` that implement the `IHandler` interface. The client code sets the chain of responsibility by calling the `SetNext` method to link the handlers together. Requests are then passed through the chain using the `HandleRequest` method until they are handled or reach the end of the chain.

