Sure, here is a simple example program that uses the canvas getImageData method:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Get Image Data Example</title>
</head>
<body>
    <canvas id="canvas" width="200" height="200"></canvas>
    <script>
        let canvas = document.getElementById('canvas');
        let ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');

        // Draw a red rectangle on the canvas
        ctx.fillStyle = 'red';
        ctx.fillRect(50, 50, 100, 100);

        // Get the image data for the entire canvas
        let imageData = ctx.getImageData(0, 0, canvas.width, canvas.height);

        // Print the pixel data to the console
        for(let i = 0; i < imageData.data.length; i+=4) {
            let red = imageData.data[i];
            let green = imageData.data[i+1];
            let blue = imageData.data[i+2];
            console.log(`Pixel ${i/4}: R=${red}, G=${green}, B=${blue}`);
        }
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

This program creates a canvas element, draws a red rectangle on it, and then uses the canvas context's getImageData method to retrieve the pixel data of the entire canvas. It then loops through the pixel data array and prints the RGB values of each pixel to the console.

