"Write a C# function called FormatIntegers that takes an enumerable of integers as input, and converts each range of consecutive integers into a sorted string representation. The string representation should include the start and end of each range, separated by a hyphen, and ranges should be separated by commas. If an integer appears multiple times in the input, it should only appear once in the output string. The function should also remove any trailing comma and space from the output string. Here is an example of the desired input and output:

Input: {5, 7, 8, 9, 10, 10, 15, 16, 17, 18, 20}

Output: 5, 7-10, 15-18, 20","revise the answer so that the last group is separated using " and " instead of ", " if all the input values are greater than or equal to 1939"

model_response:
Here is the revised C# function called FormatIntegers:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;

class Program
{
    static string FormatIntegers(IEnumerable<int> numbers)
    {
        numbers = numbers.Distinct().OrderBy(n => n);

        List<string> ranges = new List<string>();

        List<int> currentRange = new List<int>();
        foreach (int number in numbers)
        {
            if (currentRange.Count == 0 || number == currentRange.Last() + 1)
            {
                currentRange.Add(number);
            }
            else
            {
                ranges.Add(currentRange.Count > 1 ? $"{currentRange.First()}-{currentRange.Last()}" : currentRange.First().ToString());
                currentRange.Clear();
                currentRange.Add(number);
            }
        }

        if (currentRange.Count > 0)
        {
            ranges.Add(currentRange.Count > 1 ? $"{currentRange.First()}-{currentRange.Last()}" : currentRange.First().ToString());
        }

        if (numbers.All(n => n >= 1939))
        {
            return string.Join(" and ", ranges);
        }
        else
        {
            return string.Join(", ", ranges);
        }
    }

    static void Main()
    {
        IEnumerable<int> input = new int[] { 5, 7, 8, 9, 10, 10, 15, 16, 17, 18, 20 };
        string output = FormatIntegers(input);
        Console.WriteLine(output); // Output: 5, 7-10, 15-18, 20
    }
}
```

In this revised function, if all the input values are greater or equal to 1939, the last group will be separated using " and " instead of ", ".

