You can use the useEffect hook in React to run code when the component is first mounted or when certain dependencies change. To run code when the user refreshes the browser, you can use the useEffect hook with an empty dependency array. This will ensure that the code inside the useEffect hook is only run once when the component is first mounted, which includes when the page is refreshed.

Here is an example of how you can use the useEffect hook to run code when the user refreshes the browser:

```jsx
import React, { useEffect } from 'react';

const MyComponent = () => {
  useEffect(() => {
    // Code to run when the component is first mounted or when the browser is refreshed
    console.log('Component mounted or browser refreshed');
  }, []);

  return (
    <div>
      {/* Your component JSX */}
    </div>
  );
}

export default MyComponent;
```

In this example, the useEffect hook is used with an empty dependency array, which means that the code inside the hook will only run once when the component is first mounted or when the browser is refreshed. You can put any code that you want to run when the component is first mounted or when the browser is refreshed inside the useEffect hook.

Sure! Here's a simple C++ code using the SFML library to draw a smiling face in a new window:

```cpp
#include <SFML/Graphics.hpp>

int main()
{
    sf::RenderWindow window(sf::VideoMode(200, 200), "Smiling Face");

    sf::CircleShape face(80);
    face.setFillColor(sf::Color::Yellow);
    face.setOutlineColor(sf::Color::Black);
    face.setOutlineThickness(2);
    face.setPosition(50, 50);

    sf::CircleShape leftEye(10);
    leftEye.setFillColor(sf::Color::Black);
    leftEye.setPosition(80, 80);

    sf::CircleShape rightEye(10);
    rightEye.setFillColor(sf::Color::Black);
    rightEye.setPosition(140, 80);

    sf::ConvexShape mouth(3);
    mouth.setPoint(0, sf::Vector2f(100, 130));
    mouth.setPoint(1, sf::Vector2f(80, 150));
    mouth.setPoint(2, sf::Vector2f(120, 150));
    mouth.setFillColor(sf::Color::Black);

    while (window.isOpen())
    {
        sf::Event event;
        while (window.pollEvent(event))
        {
            if (event.type == sf::Event::Closed)
                window.close();
        }

        window.clear();
        window.draw(face);
        window.draw(leftEye);
        window.draw(rightEye);
        window.draw(mouth);
        window.display();
    }

    return 0;
}
```

You will need to install the SFML library and set up your C++ development environment to compile and run this code. The code creates a new window and draws a smiling face with yellow face, black eyes, and a black smiling mouth.

Here is a simple Java program for a calculator that can understand and display fractions and perform simple calculations:

```java
import java.util.Scanner;

class Fraction {
    private int numerator;
    private int denominator;

    public Fraction(int numerator, int denominator) {
        this.numerator = numerator;
        this.denominator = denominator;
    }

    public Fraction add(Fraction other) {
        int resultNumerator = this.numerator * other.denominator + other.numerator * this.denominator;
        int resultDenominator = this.denominator * other.denominator;
        return new Fraction(resultNumerator, resultDenominator);
    }

    public Fraction subtract(Fraction other) {
        int resultNumerator = this.numerator * other.denominator - other.numerator * this.denominator;
        int resultDenominator = this.denominator * other.denominator;
        return new Fraction(resultNumerator, resultDenominator);
    }

    public Fraction multiply(Fraction other) {
        int resultNumerator = this.numerator * other.numerator;
        int resultDenominator = this.denominator * other.denominator;
        return new Fraction(resultNumerator, resultDenominator);
    }

    public Fraction divide(Fraction other) {
        int resultNumerator = this.numerator * other.denominator;
        int resultDenominator = this.denominator * other.numerator;
        return new Fraction(resultNumerator, resultDenominator);
    }

    public String toString() {
        return this.numerator + "/" + this.denominator;
    }
}

public class FractionCalculator {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);

        System.out.println("Enter the first fraction (numerator denominator): ");
        int numerator1 = sc.nextInt();
        int denominator1 = sc.nextInt();

        System.out.println("Enter the second fraction (numerator denominator): ");
        int numerator2 = sc.nextInt();
        int denominator2 = sc.nextInt();

        Fraction fraction1 = new Fraction(numerator1, denominator1);
        Fraction fraction2 = new Fraction(numerator2, denominator2);

        System.out.println("Result of addition: " + fraction1.add(fraction2));
        System.out.println("Result of subtraction: " + fraction1.subtract(fraction2));
        System.out.println("Result of multiplication: " + fraction1.multiply(fraction2));
        System.out.println("Result of division: " + fraction1.divide(fraction2));

        sc.close();
    }
}
```

You can run this program to input two fractions and get the results of addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division of the fractions.

