model_response:
Here is a different approach to achieve the same goal:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    int t;
    std::cin >> t;

    for (int i = 0; i < t; ++i) {
        int X;
        std::cin >> X;

        int base = 1;
        std::vector<int> ans;
        while (X > 0) {
            int k = 1;
            while (k * (k + 1) / 2 <= X) {
                k++;
            }
            k--;
            for (int j = base + k - 1; j >= base; --j) {
                ans.push_back(j);
            }
            base += k;
            X -= k * (k + 1) / 2;
        }

        std::cout << ans.size() << std::endl;
        for (int num : ans) {
            std::cout << num << " ";
        }
        std::cout << std::endl;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

This approach also generates an array with exactly X increasing subsequences. It calculates the base and then iterates to find the next increasing subsequence by calculating the number of elements needed for that subsequence. It then adds those elements to the answer vector and repeats the process until X becomes 0. Finally, it prints the length of the array and the array itself as the output.

Certainly! Here is an example of a C# script that serializes a class's variables to its own MemoryStream in binary without using BinaryFormatter:

```csharp
using System;
using System.IO;
using System.Runtime.Serialization.Formatters.Binary;

[Serializable]
class MyClass
{
    public int intValue;
    public string stringValue;

    public void SerializeToStream(Stream stream)
    {
        BinaryWriter writer = new BinaryWriter(stream);
        writer.Write(intValue);
        writer.Write(stringValue);
    }

    public void DeserializeFromStream(Stream stream)
    {
        BinaryReader reader = new BinaryReader(stream);
        intValue = reader.ReadInt32();
        stringValue = reader.ReadString();
    }
}

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        MyClass myObject = new MyClass { intValue = 42, stringValue = "Hello World" };

        using (MemoryStream memoryStream = new MemoryStream())
        {
            myObject.SerializeToStream(memoryStream);

            memoryStream.Position = 0;

            MyClass deserializedObject = new MyClass();
            deserializedObject.DeserializeFromStream(memoryStream);

            Console.WriteLine($"Deserialized int value: {deserializedObject.intValue}");
            Console.WriteLine($"Deserialized string value: {deserializedObject.stringValue}");
        }
    }
}
```

This script defines a `MyClass` class that has two variables `intValue` and `stringValue`. The `SerializeToStream` method writes the values of these variables to a MemoryStream using a BinaryWriter, while the `DeserializeFromStream` method reads the values back from the MemoryStream using a BinaryReader.

In the `Main` method, an instance of `MyClass` is created with some sample values, serialized to a MemoryStream, then deserialized back to a new instance of `MyClass` which is then printed to the console.

This script achieves serialization of a class's variables to its own MemoryStream in binary without using BinaryFormatter.

