Here is a Haskell implementation of the two sum problem:

```haskell
import Data.List (find)

twoSum :: [Int] -> Int -> (Int, Int)
twoSum nums target = 
  let pairs = [(x, y) | x <- nums, y <- nums, x /= y, x + y == target]
  in case find (\(x, y) -> True) pairs of
    Just (x, y) -> (x, y)
    Nothing -> (-1, -1)
```

And here is a Rust implementation of the two sum problem:

```rust
use std::collections::HashMap;

fn two_sum(nums: Vec<i32>, target: i32) -> (i32, i32) {
    let mut num_map = HashMap::new();
    for (index, &num) in nums.iter().enumerate() {
        let complement = target - num;
        if let Some(&idx) = num_map.get(&complement) {
            return (nums[idx], nums[index]);
        }
        num_map.insert(num, index);
    }
    (-1, -1)
}
```

These functions take a vector of numbers and a target number as input, and return the two numbers in the array that sum to the target number. If no such numbers are found, they return (-1, -1).

Yes, there is a way to determine which sub-domain URL has been used in the controller in Symfony. You can access the current request object and retrieve the host or sub-domain from it.

To access the current request object in a controller, you can simply type hint it in the controller action method like this:

```php
use Symfony\Component\HttpFoundation\Request;

public function myAction(Request $request)
{
    $host = $request->getHost();

    // Check the host or sub-domain and perform actions accordingly
}
```

To implement this for all controllers up front, you can create a listener or middleware that checks the request object for the host or sub-domain and stores it in a parameter or attribute that can be accessed in the controller.

By creating a listener or middleware, you can make this functionality available for all controllers without having to manually check the request object in each controller action.

Here is an example of how you can create a listener to store the host or sub-domain in a parameter:

```yaml
# config/services.yaml
services:
    App\EventListener\HostListener:
        tags:
            - { name: kernel.event_subscriber }
```

```php
// src/EventListener/HostListener.php
namespace App\EventListener;

use Symfony\Component\EventDispatcher\EventSubscriberInterface;
use Symfony\Component\HttpKernel\Event\ControllerEvent;
use Symfony\Component\HttpKernel\KernelEvents;

class HostListener implements EventSubscriberInterface
{
    public function onKernelController(ControllerEvent $event)
    {
        $request = $event->getRequest();
        $host = $request->getHost();

        // Store the host in a parameter or attribute for use in the controller
        $request->attributes->set('_host', $host);
    }

    public static function getSubscribedEvents()
    {
        return [
            KernelEvents::CONTROLLER => 'onKernelController'
        ];
    }
}
```

With this listener in place, you can access the host or sub-domain in any controller by retrieving it from the request attributes:

```php
public function myAction(Request $request)
{
    $host = $request->attributes->get('_host');

    // Check the host or sub-domain and perform actions accordingly
}
```

This approach allows you to implement the functionality for determining the sub-domain URL in a centralized manner and make it available for all controllers in your Symfony application.

