Sure! Here are some examples of using jQuery to create simple galleries in just a few lines of code:

1. Basic image gallery:
```html
<div id="gallery">
  <img src="image1.jpg">
  <img src="image2.jpg">
  <img src="image3.jpg">
</div>

<script>
$(document).ready(function() {
  $('#gallery img').click(function() {
    $(this).toggleClass('selected');
  });
});
</script>
```

2. Slideshow gallery:
```html
<div id="slideshow">
  <img src="image1.jpg">
  <img src="image2.jpg">
  <img src="image3.jpg">
</div>

<script>
$(document).ready(function() {
  $('#slideshow img:gt(0)').hide();
  setInterval(function() {
    $('#slideshow :first-child').fadeOut().next('img').fadeIn().end().appendTo('#slideshow');
  }, 2000);
});
</script>
```

3. Filterable gallery:
```html
<button class="filter" data-filter="all">All</button>
<button class="filter" data-filter=".nature">Nature</button>
<button class="filter" data-filter=".animals">Animals</button>

<div id="gallery">
  <img class="nature" src="nature1.jpg">
  <img class="animals" src="animals1.jpg">
  <img class="nature" src="nature2.jpg">
  <img class="animals" src="animals2.jpg">
</div>

<script>
$(document).ready(function() {
  $('.filter').click(function() {
    var filter = $(this).data('filter');
    $('#gallery img').hide();
    $(filter).show();
  });
});
</script>
``` 

These are just a few examples of how jQuery can be used to create simple and effective galleries with just a few lines of code.

