The best method for handling an "or" expression is to use the `|` operator in regular expressions. For example, if you want to match either "apple" or "banana", you can use the regex pattern `/(apple|banana)/`.

Unfortunately, when dealing with a large number of elements in an "or" expression in JavaScript, there is no more efficient syntax than writing out each element explicitly separated by `|`. This can be tedious and prone to errors, especially with a large number of elements. 

One alternative approach would be to programmatically generate the regex pattern using a loop or function, but this may not necessarily be more efficient than writing out the elements manually.

Here is an example SwiftUI view that displays accelerometerData, gyroscopeData, magnetometerData, and ambientLightData of a Mac device using CoreMotion and CoreHaptics:

```swift
import SwiftUI
import CoreMotion
import CoreHaptics

struct SensorDataView: View {
    @State private var accelerometerData: String = ""
    @State private var gyroscopeData: String = ""
    @State private var magnetometerData: String = ""
    @State private var ambientLightData: String = ""

    let motionManager = CMMotionManager()
    let lightSensor = CLSensors()

    var body: some View {
        VStack {
            Text("Accelerometer Data: \(accelerometerData)")
            Text("Gyroscope Data: \(gyroscopeData)")
            Text("Magnetometer Data: \(magnetometerData)")
            Text("Ambient Light Data: \(ambientLightData)")
        }
        .onAppear {
            if motionManager.isAccelerometerAvailable {
                motionManager.accelerometerUpdateInterval = 0.1
                motionManager.startAccelerometerUpdates(to: .main) { data, error in
                    guard let data = data else { return }
                    self.accelerometerData = "x: \(data.acceleration.x), y: \(data.acceleration.y), z: \(data.acceleration.z)"
                }
            }

            if motionManager.isGyroAvailable {
                motionManager.gyroUpdateInterval = 0.1
                motionManager.startGyroUpdates(to: .main) { data, error in
                    guard let data = data else { return }
                    self.gyroscopeData = "x: \(data.rotationRate.x), y: \(data.rotationRate.y), z: \(data.rotationRate.z)"
                }
            }

            if motionManager.isMagnetometerAvailable {
                motionManager.startMagnetometerUpdates(to: .main) { data, error in
                    guard let data = data else { return }
                    self.magnetometerData = "x: \(data.magneticField.x), y: \(data.magneticField.y), z: \(data.magneticField.z)"
                }
            }

            if lightSensor.isAmbientLightSensorAvailable {
                lightSensor.startAmbientLightUpdates(to: .main) { data, error in
                    guard let data = data else { return }
                    self.ambientLightData = "\(data.ambientLightLevel)"
                }
            }
        }
        .onDisappear {
            motionManager.stopAccelerometerUpdates()
            motionManager.stopGyroUpdates()
            motionManager.stopMagnetometerUpdates()
            lightSensor.stopAmbientLightUpdates()
        }
    }
}

struct SensorDataView_Previews: PreviewProvider {
    static var previews: some View {
        SensorDataView()
    }
}
```

Don't forget to import necessary frameworks and make sure you have the appropriate permissions set up in your app's Info.plist file for accessing motion and light sensor data.

