1. `std::unique_ptr`: This smart pointer is used for managing a single object. It ensures that only one `unique_ptr` can own the object at a time, and when the `unique_ptr` goes out of scope, it automatically deletes the object it points to.

```cpp
std::unique_ptr<int> ptr(new int(5));
```

2. `std::shared_ptr`: This smart pointer is used for managing objects that are shared by multiple pointers. It keeps a reference count of how many `shared_ptr` objects are pointing to the same object, and the object is deleted when the last `shared_ptr` goes out of scope.

```cpp
std::shared_ptr<int> ptr1 = std::make_shared<int>(10);
std::shared_ptr<int> ptr2 = ptr1;
```

3. `std::weak_ptr`: This smart pointer is used in conjunction with `shared_ptr` to break potential circular references. It provides a non-owning reference to an object managed by a `shared_ptr` without affecting the reference count.

```cpp
std::shared_ptr<int> ptr = std::make_shared<int>(15);
std::weak_ptr<int> weakPtr = ptr;
```

These are some basic examples of C++ smart pointers that can help manage memory in a safe and efficient way.

