You can use the following function in C# to find duplicates in a collection:

```c#
public List<T> FindDuplicates<T>(IEnumerable<T> collection)
{
    List<T> duplicates = new List<T>();
    HashSet<T> set = new HashSet<T>();

    foreach (T item in collection)
    {
        if (!set.Add(item))
        {
            duplicates.Add(item);
        }
    }

    return duplicates;
}
```

You can call this function with your collection of items to find duplicates. It will return a list of items that are duplicates in the collection.

Sure, here is a simple example in C# to calculate the value of PI using the Leibniz formula:

```csharp
using System;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        int iterations = 1000000;
        decimal pi = CalculatePi(iterations);

        Console.WriteLine($"Value of PI after {iterations} iterations: {pi}");
    }

    static decimal CalculatePi(int iterations)
    {
        decimal pi = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < iterations; i++)
        {
            decimal denominator = 1 + 2 * i;
            if (i % 2 == 0)
            {
                pi += 4 / denominator;
            }
            else
            {
                pi -= 4 / denominator;
            }
        }
        return pi;
    }
}
```

This code uses the Leibniz formula to estimate the value of PI using a specified number of iterations. The more iterations you use, the more accurate the value of PI will be.

Here is an example of a generic Java method that waits for a condition with a maximum number of attempts:

```java
public void waitForCondition(int maxAttempts, Condition condition) {
    int attempts = 0;

    while (attempts < maxAttempts) {
        if (condition.check()) {
            System.out.println("Condition met");
            break;
        } else {
            attempts++;
            System.out.println("Condition not met, attempting again...");
            try {
                Thread.sleep(1000); // Wait for 1 second before attempting again
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                e.printStackTrace();
            }
        }
    }

    if (attempts == maxAttempts) {
        System.out.println("Maximum attempts reached, condition not met");
    }
}

interface Condition {
    boolean check();
}
```

You can call this method by passing in the maximum number of attempts and a `Condition` object that implements the `check()` method to determine if the condition is met. This method will wait for the condition to be met with a maximum number of attempts before giving up.

Here is a sample C++ program that reads a text file line by line and counts the number of occurrences of a specific word in the file:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <fstream>
#include <string>

using namespace std;

int main() {
    string filename;
    string searchWord;

    cout << "Enter the name of the file: ";
    cin >> filename;

    cout << "Enter the word to search for: ";
    cin >> searchWord;

    ifstream file(filename);
    if (!file.is_open()) {
        cerr << "Error opening file" << endl;
        return 1;
    }

    string line;
    int count = 0;
    while (getline(file, line)) {
        size_t pos = 0;
        while ((pos = line.find(searchWord, pos)) != string::npos) {
            count++;
            pos += searchWord.length();
        }
    }

    cout << "The word \"" << searchWord << "\" occurs " << count << " times in the file." << endl;

    file.close();

    return 0;
}
```

To use this program, simply compile and run it in a C++ environment. The program will prompt you to enter the name of the text file to read and the word to search for. It will then read the file line by line, count the occurrences of the specified word, and print out the result.

