Here is an example of how you can create unit tests for the given C# string extension method:

```csharp
using Xunit;

public class StringExtensionsTests
{
    [Fact]
    public void Clean_RemovesSuffixes()
    {
        // Arrange
        string input = "hello_world";
        string[] suffixesToClean = new string[] { "_world", "hello_" };

        // Act
        string result = input.clean(suffixesToClean);

        // Assert
        Assert.Equal("", result);
    }

    [Fact]
    public void Clean_DoesNotAffectStringWithoutSuffixes()
    {
        // Arrange
        string input = "hello_world";
        string[] suffixesToClean = new string[] { "foo", "bar" };

        // Act
        string result = input.clean(suffixesToClean);

        // Assert
        Assert.Equal("hello_world", result);
    }

    [Fact]
    public void Clean_RemovesMultipleSuffixes()
    {
        // Arrange
        string input = "hello_world";
        string[] suffixesToClean = new string[] { "_world", "hello" };

        // Act
        string result = input.clean(suffixesToClean);

        // Assert
        Assert.Equal("", result);
    }
}
```

In this example, we have created unit tests using xUnit to test the `clean` method. The first test checks if the method correctly removes the suffixes specified in the input string. The second test checks if the method does not affect the string if no suffixes are found. The third test checks if the method correctly removes multiple suffixes at once. 

You can run these tests to verify the functionality of the `clean` method.

