In React with TypeScript, you can achieve this by first filtering through the array of children to get the specific type of child you want, then adding a classname to that specific type of child.

Here is an example of how you can do this:

```tsx
import React, { ReactNode } from 'react';

interface Props {
  children?: ReactNode;
}

const Component = ({ children }: Props) => {
  // Filter through the children to get a specific type (e.g., div)
  const filteredChildren = React.Children.toArray(children).filter(child => React.isValidElement(child) && child.type === 'div');

  // Add a classname to the filtered children
  const childrenWithClassName = React.Children.map(filteredChildren, child => {
    return React.cloneElement(child, { className: 'your-custom-classname' });
  });

  return <div>{childrenWithClassName}</div>;
};

export default Component;
```

In this example, we first convert the children to an array using `React.Children.toArray()` and then use the `filter` method to only keep the elements that are of a specific type (in this case, `div`). Next, we use `React.Children.map()` to iterate over the filtered children and add a custom classname to each child using `React.cloneElement()`.

You can then use this `Component` in your parent component and pass in the children as props. The children of the specific type (`div` in this case) will have the custom classname applied.

